PASS level_intents: all 66 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 6.8/10 (bar 8), 1 level(s) below 6/10: Strong spine: real kabaddi verbs, a clear one-idea-per-unit ladder, and chapter finales built on mid-match reversals (L55, L61, L9) that really do test reading. Four problems hold it back. (1) The READ rows show the held or stop % on every gem, so many puzzles reduce to picking the lowest number, and that undermines the 'read the formation' fantasy. (2) Defend Stands are a telegraph-then-matching-hold loop repeated about 16 times, with L35/L36/L39, L37/L59, L46/L63 and L26/L47 close to duplicates. (3) Difficulty is uneven. Some teach and breather units are harder than spikes or bosses (L45, L46, L47, L63, L40, L41), and two units have unreachable 3-star targets. (4) Several units show almost no skill gap between casual and skilled players (L26, L35, L2), so the outcome is mostly luck. The meta loop (auction, squad, ghosts, ladder, kit album) is a good retention base, but the content set only half-uses it. The auction units are shallow, and nothing in the 66 trials teaches the async ghost-defence or the team-builder as ongoing play. Curve: Ch1 (L1-11) is a clean, well-paced onboarding: touch, home, hold, cover, then a short boss. The boss (L11, 95% skilled) is easier than the L10 spike. Ch2 (L12-22) adds Toe, Dubki, Block, bonus and Do-or-Die in a sensible order, with the breather at L21 and a boss at L22 that is heavy (held 62-85%, 21 raids). Ch3 (L23-33) is good, but difficulty ramps fast: skilled win rates of 70-89% and mid-skill rates of 70-74% are common by L27-L31. Ch4 (L34-44) mixes in auction units that play differently from the rest. L40 (55% casual) is the hardest unit so far and sits inside the Ch4 spike slot, harder than its boss L44 (67%). Ch5 (L45-55) gets unfair at the start: L45 (55% mid), L46 and L47 (59%) are teach units below the pass rates a lesson should have. Ch5 ends well with L55. Ch6 piles systems on top of each other (fatigue, DRS, feints, captain, formation), and L63, a breather, is the second-hardest mid-skill result in the chapter. Spike, breather and boss placement is mostly right. The chapter-closing reversal bosses are the best moments. Problems: difficulty is carried by inflated grip values rather than new decisions; Defend Stands repeat the same loop; most chapters add a mechanic and then immediately combine it; and the 2-star ceiling on L50 and L53 shows the star system is not tuned. For retention, the design promises ghosts, the ladder, weekly themes, the auction and the Kit Album, but few trials use the team-builder, upgrades or weekly formation themes as ongoing reasons to replay. Add a replayable 'trial remix' per chapter (changed formation, same lesson) and tie squad upgrades visibly to trial difficulty.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L63 (5/10): A breather with 57% mid, 30/40 competent; fails the breather role. → Widen the chain window and lower the goal to 1 chain; Easy brain.
L19 (6/10): Raider lands 85%, competent only 30/40, mid 73%; two systems; star 'all 7 on mat' is hard when you start 6. → Lower touch land to ~78%; reduce lose-condition to <3 on mat; restate the star.
L21 (6/10): Not a breather (76% mid). The ghost has no interaction, so it's a score chase; bonus gem 5% then free. → Show the ghost replay plus its mistakes; ease the target to 3 points.
L26 (6/10): Skilled 78 vs casual 76: luck, not skill. Raider two touches and all-out threat on a Medium brain. → Lower the brain to Easy; make the cover choice measurable (stop % higher for the right defender).
L35 (6/10): Near duplicate of L4/L6 with a role multiplier; casual 73% vs skilled 81%. → Merge with L36 or add switching under time pressure.
L40 (6/10): 55% casual, 68% mid; hardest unit so far, harder than the boss; 'counter for every move' is a lookup under 5+5 raids. → Lower grip to 0.75 and 4 raids each.
L41 (6/10): Raider s 0.10 trivial, yet only 61% mid; the auction decides; a pass-or-fail from budget math. → Allow purchasing a second-best path and soften the tight purse.
L45 (6/10): 55% mid; walk-home 47% held at 1 touch; teach unit is a coin flip. → Lower grip to 0.8 and show a preview of timer budget.
L46 (6/10): Mid 63%, 59% casual; chain window needs two taps; 30/40 competent. → Widen the chain window for this lesson; lower raider skill.
L47 (6/10): Duplicates L26 with chain; 59% casual; losing two men on a miss is harsh. → Merge into L26 or start with 4 on mat.
L50 (6/10): Best 3★ is 2★ (3-star unreachable); DRS is a coin-flip percentage, and the mat needs scouting. → Fix the star criteria; make the DRS verdict legible (replay shows the touch).
L53 (6/10): Best 2★ (3-star not achieved), 59% casual; DRS plus Super Raid plus ghost target. → Fix the star; drop one gimmick.
L56 (6/10): Overloaded: fatigue plus chain plus super raid, and a 'win by 4' target with 11 raids; 30/40. → One verb only, win by 3, preview the stamina bar.
L60 (6/10): Too many systems (rotation, captain, reversal, scouting, feints, 14 raids). → Remove feints and scouting.
L62 (6/10): Walk 81% held, no Dash, plus feints, DRS, scouting; 66% mid; punitive. → Allow Dash with a wall hint and cut feints.
L1 (7/10): 99% casual win, 4 optimal moves, nearly impossible to fail. The 'touch only counts when home' lesson is never demonstrated by a failure. → Make the first raid show the lost touch if the player stops short or is held (scripted rookie grab). Keep the 3-star criterion.
L5 (7/10): Star 'Win within 2 raids' for 4 points needs two 2-touch raids on a mat where the second touch is risky; practically unreachable. Mid-skill 83%. → Change the star to 'Score 2-touch raid once' or lower to 'within 3 raids'.
L7 (7/10): Struggle mini-game outcome is 45% RNG (pull %); the lesson is 'raid again' but the star 'Break free' is luck-driven. Competent 35/40. → Make the pull-away timing window drive the %; show window width in the UI and tie the star to a perfect timing.
L9 (7/10): Intent text says T's guard both corners but mat 'T o o T o T o' has a rookie at slot 7. Also combines hold-match, return tackle, and reversal at once. → Fix the mat or text (T o o T o T T), and drop 'return tackle' from the mechanics list.
L10 (7/10): Harder than the chapter boss (76% mid vs 90%). 'Two points is a lot' misdescribes: from 11-13 you need +3 net. → Fix the copy; raise the start to 12-13 or ease the Village captain slightly; swap L10 and L11 difficulty.
L11 (7/10): Easy brain, s 0.50; 95% skilled. Anticlimax after the spike; the greedy personality is a gift not a test. → Make Bholu Medium and add a half-time shape change; 3 stars at +6 should be hard.
L16 (7/10): Packs bonus-free counter with toe, dubki and block defence; the key lesson is hidden by a 4+4 match. → Make it 3 raids with a visible empty-raid counter highlighted and drop defence.
L22 (7/10): Held rates 62-85%; grip 1.13 feels like punishment; 69% mid, 21 raids. → Cut to ~14 raids or lower the grip; keep the empty-raid clock as the test.
L24 (7/10): Two lessons (dubki home plus lunge/pull); held 71% on walk; lunge 40% equals pull 40%, so no real choice. → Make lunge strong shallow (50%) and weak deep (30%) so the choice is real.
L25 (7/10): Mid 69% (casual 69%); rush windows unclear. → Show the rush range ring; lower the hop land rate.

level-design score 6.8/10 — Strong spine: real kabaddi verbs, a clear one-idea-per-unit ladder, and chapter finales built on mid-match reversals (L55, L61, L9) that really do test reading. Four problems hold it back. (1) The READ rows show the held or stop % on every gem, so many puzzles reduce to picking the lowest number, and that undermines the 'read the formation' fantasy. (2) Defend Stands are a telegraph-then-matching-hold loop repeated about 16 times, with L35/L36/L39, L37/L59, L46/L63 and L26/L47 close to duplicates. (3) Difficulty is uneven. Some teach and breather units are harder than spikes or bosses (L45, L46, L47, L63, L40, L41), and two units have unreachable 3-star targets. (4) Several units show almost no skill gap between casual and skilled players (L26, L35, L2), so the outcome is mostly luck. The meta loop (auction, squad, ghosts, ladder, kit album) is a good retention base, but the content set only half-uses it. The auction units are shallow, and nothing in the 66 trials teaches the async ghost-defence or the team-builder as ongoing play.
curve: Ch1 (L1-11) is a clean, well-paced onboarding: touch, home, hold, cover, then a short boss. The boss (L11, 95% skilled) is easier than the L10 spike. Ch2 (L12-22) adds Toe, Dubki, Block, bonus and Do-or-Die in a sensible order, with the breather at L21 and a boss at L22 that is heavy (held 62-85%, 21 raids). Ch3 (L23-33) is good, but difficulty ramps fast: skilled win rates of 70-89% and mid-skill rates of 70-74% are common by L27-L31. Ch4 (L34-44) mixes in auction units that play differently from the rest. L40 (55% casual) is the hardest unit so far and sits inside the Ch4 spike slot, harder than its boss L44 (67%). Ch5 (L45-55) gets unfair at the start: L45 (55% mid), L46 and L47 (59%) are teach units below the pass rates a lesson should have. Ch5 ends well with L55. Ch6 piles systems on top of each other (fatigue, DRS, feints, captain, formation), and L63, a breather, is the second-hardest mid-skill result in the chapter. Spike, breather and boss placement is mostly right. The chapter-closing reversal bosses are the best moments. Problems: difficulty is carried by inflated grip values rather than new decisions; Defend Stands repeat the same loop; most chapters add a mechanic and then immediately combine it; and the 2-star ceiling on L50 and L53 shows the star system is not tuned. For retention, the design promises ghosts, the ladder, weekly themes, the auction and the Kit Album, but few trials use the team-builder, upgrades or weekly formation themes as ongoing reasons to replay. Add a replayable 'trial remix' per chapter (changed formation, same lesson) and tie squad upgrades visibly to trial difficulty.
